    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Bleeding after intercourse and between periods, with heavier periods, for about four months, with tiredness and a dull pelvic ache. Never had cervical screening. Speculum examination shows a friable, exophytic mass about 4 cm across on the cervix that bleeds on contact. Punch biopsy, HPV test and cytology taken; urgent gynecologic oncology consultation booked</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>N93.8</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem linked to her documented abnormal bleeding with anemia; superseded by the diagnosis at the consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Iron deficiency anemia from chronic blood loss: hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL, mean cell volume 72 fL, ferritin 6 ng/mL, with tiredness and breathlessness on stairs</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>D50.0</td><td>S — Synthetic modern finding linked to her documented anemia from vaginal bleeding.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix, HPV 16 positive: an exophytic tumor about 4.5 cm across with clinical involvement of the left parametrium, not reaching the pelvic wall; pelvic MRI and PET-CT ordered for FIGO 2018 staging. Synthetic modern episode linked to her documented cervical cancer</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C53.9</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the gynecologic oncology consultation.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

