<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix (C53.9). Invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix related to HPV 16, about 4.5 cm with clinical spread into the left parametrium: at least FIGO 2018 stage IIB on examination. FIGO 2018 allows imaging to assign stage: a pelvic MRI defines local extent and an FDG PET-CT looks for nodal and distant spread, which would change the radiation fields and the stage. With parametrial spread, radical hysterectomy is not advised; the standard treatment, with intent to cure, is pelvic external beam radiation with weekly cisplatin, followed by image-guided brachytherapy, completed within eight weeks, because a longer overall time lowers the chance of cure. Kidney function allows cisplatin. HIV testing is recommended at diagnosis. Chemoradiation will end her ability to carry a pregnancy and will very likely end ovarian function; egg or embryo freezing needs about two weeks of stimulation and would delay treatment slightly, and laparoscopic ovarian transposition can sometimes preserve hormone function; any later pregnancy would need a gestational carrier. The decision is hers. Bleeding is now light; if it becomes heavy, vaginal packing or a few early radiation fractions control it. Anemia should be corrected before radiation with iron, and hemoglobin watched weekly during it. Acetaminophen is the right analgesic; anti-inflammatory painkillers should be avoided with cisplatin. The diagnosis was given to her first, in her language, and she asked to hear everything; I explained the plan to her directly and answered each of her questions honestly. [S]</item><item>Iron deficiency anemia (D50.0). From chronic tumor bleeding; oral iron is reasonable now that the bleeding is lighter, with transfusion only if hemoglobin falls sharply or she has symptoms. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Barragán&apos;s intake note. Cervical punch biopsy: invasive squamous cell carcinoma. HPV test positive for HPV 16; cytology with squamous cancer cells. Hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL, ferritin 6 ng/mL. Creatinine 0.7 mg/dL, eGFR 117 today. PHQ-9 9 and GAD-7 9 today. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Pelvic MRI and FDG PET-CT within ten days to assign the FIGO 2018 stage. [S]</item><item>HIV and hepatitis C tests now. [S]</item><item>If staging confirms stage IIB without nodal or distant spread: pelvic external beam radiation 45 Gy in 25 fractions with cisplatin 40 mg/m2 weekly, then image-guided high-dose-rate brachytherapy, all within eight weeks. [S]</item><item>Before treatment, her decision about egg or embryo freezing (reproductive endocrinology within days if she wants it) or ovarian transposition; treatment should not be delayed beyond about two weeks. [S]</item><item>Continue ferrous sulfate and acetaminophen; no anti-inflammatory painkillers during cisplatin; weekly blood count, creatinine and magnesium during treatment. [S]</item><item>Oncology social worker from today; PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at diagnosis, before treatment, mid-treatment and after. [S]</item><item>All discussions with her directly, in Spanish with a professional interpreter when the clinician does not speak Spanish; information shared only with the people she chooses. [S]</item><item>After treatment: PET-CT at about three months, a vaginal dilator, and hormone therapy if ovarian failure occurs. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Barragán, who was present. The diagnosis, the staging plan and the likely treatment were explained to Ms. Perón in Spanish through the interpreter, and she was given written information in Spanish. She chose to tell her family herself; no one else has been given information. She decides who receives this note. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Gynecologic oncology review with the staging results in about two weeks, jointly with Dr. Barragán, to confirm the stage and her treatment and fertility decisions. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
